Ray Britton is with Troop 42 in Oak Ridge, Tennessee, and he wrote in to say

196
00:20:47,280 --> 00:20:53,040
recently: we found that there are a number of new families who expect scout camping to be

197
00:20:53,040 --> 00:20:59,760
family trips, where mom, dad and the siblings of the scout come along. We pointed out that this

198
00:20:59,760 --> 00:21:04,639
really wasn't the way Boy Scouts camp and this made some of the families feel unwelcome to

199
00:21:04,640 --> 00:21:10,160
participate at all. We had our first visiting weeblos for the year already and the question came up

200
00:21:10,160 --> 00:21:15,680
about parents participating in camping. I found myself struggling a little to articulate that

201
00:21:15,680 --> 00:21:22,640
Boy Scout camping is not really family camping, while still saying we want and need parent

202
00:21:22,640 --> 00:21:28,320
participation. How do you suggest we handle this? Well, Ray, you'll head a lot of this off at the

203
00:21:28,320 --> 00:21:35,040
pass by explaining the basics to visiting parents and by being very specific, and this is the way that

204
00:21:35,040 --> 00:21:41,520
I would put it. First, scouting is a progressive program. In Cub Scouts, boys work closely with

205
00:21:41,520 --> 00:21:47,520
their families. in weeblos they work closely with their adult leaders. in scouts they work

206
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:52,240
together with some adult oversight and in venturing they set and administer their own

207
00:21:52,240 --> 00:21:58,960
program. So there is a progression to all of this. It's not the same in Cub Scouts as it is

208
00:21:58,960 --> 00:22:06,000
in weeblos or scouts or venturing. The second point is: scout camping is not family camping.

209
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:11,600
When scouts go camping they are working and camping together as patrols. Adults all camp

210
00:22:11,600 --> 00:22:16,800
separately. We don't share tents with our sons and we may have little or no interaction with

211
00:22:16,800 --> 00:22:22,800
our sons on the course of a camping trip. Third point is: only parents or guardians and

212
00:22:22,800 --> 00:22:28,399
registered trained adult volunteers are permitted on camping trips. No younger or older siblings

213
00:22:28,399 --> 00:22:34,480
are permitted unless they're registered members of the scout troop. As a rule of thumb we maintain

214
00:22:34,480 --> 00:22:39,840
an adult to scout ratio to maximum of four scouts to every adult. Now, before I go on,

215
00:22:39,840 --> 00:22:44,560
that's called a rule of thumb. It's not a set written policy. It's just something that we

216
00:22:44,560 --> 00:22:50,480
look at and we say: well, that's the maximum number of adults that we really need to have

217
00:22:50,480 --> 00:22:54,960
or want to have along on a camping trip. Your mileage is going to vary on that.

218
00:22:54,960 --> 00:23:00,000
I'm just telling you what I prefer to do. Registered trained adult volunteers have the

219
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:05,040
first refusal for open spots on all trips. Parents and guardians will be welcome to

220
00:23:05,040 --> 00:23:10,480
take any remaining spots. Now, you may agree with that, that way of doing things, or not,

221
00:23:10,480 --> 00:23:16,720
but in saying that we try to maintain this ratio at a maximum, it gives everybody the idea that

222
00:23:16,720 --> 00:23:23,200
there's something more going on than camping just being a way for scouts and adults to interact

223
00:23:23,200 --> 00:23:28,560
with each other or for parents to come along and go camping with their sons. Fourth, we say that

224
00:23:28,560 --> 00:23:33,120
parents who wish to accompany their scouts regularly on camping trips are encouraged to

225
00:23:33,120 --> 00:23:38,480
become registered trained adult volunteers. Now, those four things set a baseline of

226
00:23:38,480 --> 00:23:45,120
understanding. It's not that we don't welcome parents to participate, it's just that scout camping

227
00:23:45,120 --> 00:23:50,960
is a different thing. The committee chair can describe what is otherwise expected of parents

228
00:23:50,960 --> 00:23:57,600
and families during their visit, and that will give them some idea of how their role is changing

229
00:23:57,600 --> 00:24:03,280
as their boys progress through scouting. And then I would go on to offer you this advice:

230
00:24:03,280 --> 00:24:07,680
If I have a number of new adults on a camping trip, I get them together and explain something

231
00:24:07,680 --> 00:24:14,800
that you and other listeners have probably heard me say many times before. I say that while we're camping,

232
00:24:15,520 --> 00:24:21,920
I am the scout master and I am the leader of the adults. Just to be clear about this. I expect

233
00:24:21,920 --> 00:24:28,800
you to follow my directions. Your sons have elected their own leaders and they'll be following

234
00:24:28,800 --> 00:24:33,440
the directions of their own leaders. and doing what scouts do? I have a couple of basic ground

235
00:24:33,440 --> 00:24:39,280
rules. We adults have an area assigned to us. that's where we put up our tents, and I'm going to

236
00:24:39,280 --> 00:24:45,200
ask you to stay in that area Now, like just about every other parent who's been in your place before,

237
00:24:45,200 --> 00:24:50,160
this is going to make you a little uncomfortable, but you'll begin to understand why this is important

238
00:24:50,160 --> 00:24:55,520
as time goes by. And then I have these three important things that I want to ask of you.

239
00:24:55,520 --> 00:25:01,120
If your son asks you a question, I'd appreciate if you reply with the following answer:

240
00:25:01,120 --> 00:25:06,720
go ask your patrol leader and leave it at that. If your son walks away from his patrol to come and

241
00:25:06,720 --> 00:25:12,240
find you, I would like you to ask him where his patrol is and then tell him that's where he

242
00:25:12,240 --> 00:25:18,160
needs to be and leave it at that. Now, if you see or hear something that concerns you, I'll ask that

243
00:25:18,160 --> 00:25:23,680
you come to me and talk to me and let me handle it, unless what you see or hear is going to

244
00:25:23,680 --> 00:25:29,600
lead to injury before you can get to me. Our youth leaders are very respectful of adults,

245
00:25:29,600 --> 00:25:38,000
and even an offhand comment from you may end up misdirecting them. so anytime scouts need direction,

246
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:44,160
it's going to come from me, the scout master, And before we leave, let me ask you that if you've

247
00:25:44,160 --> 00:25:49,920
packed any gear in your stuff that your son is going to need, I need you to unpack it and give

248
00:25:49,920 --> 00:25:54,960
it to him now, before we leave, so he won't have to find you later on and he won't be distracted

249
00:25:54,960 --> 00:26:01,920
from following his own leaders and working with his patrol. Now most folks get it. myself and my

250
00:26:01,920 --> 00:26:09,520
assistant scout masters are always ready to throw a friendly, kind and courteous block at a parent who

251
00:26:09,520 --> 00:26:15,600
leaves the adult area and starts heading for scout country on a camping trip, and we explain

252
00:26:15,600 --> 00:26:20,800
that someone else had to do this favor for us. in the past, I've always been able to safely

253
00:26:20,800 --> 00:26:26,240
tell new parents that, unless at some point they are not at least a little upset or uncertain about

254
00:26:26,240 --> 00:26:32,399
how we do things, we're probably not doing our job correctly, because scouting isn't like anything else

255
00:26:32,399 --> 00:26:37,520
and it takes some time to understand how it works. and it doesn't work like you probably think

256
00:26:37,520 --> 00:26:43,680
that it does. So finally, Ray, I'd say that an annual family camping trip is usually a good

257
00:26:43,680 --> 00:26:49,200
idea for any troop. This allows parents and siblings to get out with the scouts once,

258
00:26:49,200 --> 00:26:54,560
and that's usually enough for most. but if we give people who really want to do that the opportunity

259
00:26:54,560 --> 00:26:59,840
to do that and we make that the program for one of the camping trips during the year, then that

260
00:26:59,840 --> 00:27:05,760
usually satisfies their desire to get out and go camping with their sons. For this next question,

261
00:27:05,760 --> 00:27:12,320
I was asked by the center that they remain anonymous. does your troop have bylaws? I have

262
00:27:12,320 --> 00:27:17,520
a family who's recently joined our troop that thinks that we should. They seem to be the type-

263
00:27:17,520 --> 00:27:22,400
and you probably know them well- that create controversies and try to solve them by suggesting

264
00:27:22,400 --> 00:27:28,879
that we adopt lots of rules. They prepared a huge document with the added bonus of things like stating

265
00:27:28,879 --> 00:27:34,720
that new parents are required to come on x number of camping trips for the first year. I find these

266
00:27:34,720 --> 00:27:38,960
conversations at the committee meetings are distracting to me and everyone else, and they

267
00:27:38,960 --> 00:27:44,080
seem to be scaring other parents away from participating. So, besides the initial question

268
00:27:44,080 --> 00:27:49,280
about having bylaws, how do you and your committee chair handle these sorts of parents?

269
00:27:49,280 --> 00:27:54,080
Well, my troop does not have any bylaws. We don't have any rule book. We don't have a

270
00:27:54,080 --> 00:27:59,520
procedures, manual or anything of the kind. If bylaws and rule books and policy manuals

271
00:27:59,520 --> 00:28:05,120
and things like that were useful in the past century of scouting, we'd be trained to use

272
00:28:05,120 --> 00:28:09,520
that sort of thing Now. do you recall anything even remotely resembling this in training?

273
00:28:10,160 --> 00:28:15,600
I don't. So once again, no bylaws, policy manuals, rule books, things like that.

274
00:28:16,480 --> 00:28:20,879
You know, if you want to familiarize new families with the way that the program happens,

275
00:28:20,879 --> 00:28:26,000
you can do that on one sheet of eight and a half bylaw and paper. You might need two sides, but

276
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:31,760
if you're smart you can keep it to one side. So how do we deal with parents like this?

277
00:28:32,480 --> 00:28:38,720
So you asked my advice about how to stop parents from interfering like this.

278
00:28:39,280 --> 00:28:45,440
Before I brand everything like this as an interference, I will accept the premise that

279
00:28:45,440 --> 00:28:49,600
they're trying to be helpful. They may not understand that it's interfering and we need

280
00:28:49,600 --> 00:28:54,480
to be very sensitive to the idea of things that we hear that we don't particularly want to hear.

281
00:28:55,360 --> 00:29:01,040
that may be indications that we're getting something wrong. So before I go in and I start

282
00:29:01,040 --> 00:29:07,600
telling you how to deal with people who interfere, let's be sure that I don't accept the premise

283
00:29:07,600 --> 00:29:12,160
that everybody who has something to say that might cross the grain of the troop committee

284
00:29:12,160 --> 00:29:18,400
or the scout master is necessarily interfering, but if it's determined, they do. the only real

285
00:29:18,400 --> 00:29:23,840
way to deal with them is to tell them to stop. My troop committee chair is able to do this very

286
00:29:23,840 --> 00:29:29,920
well. Now, you're not necessarily going to end up being polite about this, because people who

287
00:29:29,920 --> 00:29:35,440
interfere run over polite people in a few seconds, but my committee chair draws a very

288
00:29:35,440 --> 00:29:40,800
specific line and tells them that that's the end of the matter and if they don't stop,

289
00:29:40,800 --> 00:29:45,280
they'll be removed from the troop along with their son. There's not going to be any arguing or

290
00:29:45,280 --> 00:29:52,000
equivocating. that sounds pretty harsh, doesn't it? Well, you can wait six months to get to the

291
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:55,680
point where you have to draw that line very specifically, after you've been polite and they've

292
00:29:55,680 --> 00:30:01,360
run over you for six months, or you can just draw it to begin with. I would suggest that you

293
00:30:01,360 --> 00:30:06,400
just draw it to begin with. If they get it and they understand what you've said,

294
00:30:06,400 --> 00:30:11,120
you've solved your problem. Unfortunately, some people don't- it's a small percentage, but they

295
00:30:11,120 --> 00:30:15,200
won't- and once you've drawn the line, they're going to cross it in a few hours or days and you'll

296
00:30:15,200 --> 00:30:22,240
have to tell them to leave, and that's unfortunate. Now, in 30 years I've seen this happen maybe once

297
00:30:22,240 --> 00:30:28,160
or twice, so this is not necessarily a common thing. when it needs to happen, it needs to happen

298
00:30:28,160 --> 00:30:33,920
quickly, and sometimes the best way to do it is just to get them out of the picture. Scouting is

299
00:30:33,920 --> 00:30:38,640
constituted to put absolute decision-making power into the hands of the committee chair and the

300
00:30:38,640 --> 00:30:44,240
chartered organization representative. Any volunteer or any member of any scout unit,

301
00:30:44,240 --> 00:30:49,920
and thus their family, can be removed at any time, with or without cause. Nobody needs to vote.

302
00:30:49,920 --> 00:30:55,600
there's no quasi-judicial proceedings. there's just a letter with one sentence: as of this date,

303
00:30:55,600 --> 00:31:01,840
your registration is terminated, and there's no appeal of that decision. Now, naturally, most

304
00:31:01,840 --> 00:31:06,000
committee chairs and chartered organization representatives and scoutmasters and all

305
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:11,280
the wonderful people who are involved in scouting are going to run things based on consensus

306
00:31:11,280 --> 00:31:17,280
and goodwill. but you'll also need to have a backbone to stand up to people who cause trouble

307
00:31:17,280 --> 00:31:22,880
and you have to tell them to stop or to leave. and this all sounds very drastic. I know

308
00:31:22,880 --> 00:31:29,840
Too many adults who interfere inappropriately, kill scouting for boys, and it's up to the other

309
00:31:29,840 --> 00:31:35,520
adults involved to protect the scouts from this sort of thing. The only mistake that you can

310
00:31:35,520 --> 00:31:41,680
make in a circumstance like this is not ending it soon enough. nobody wants to confront things

311
00:31:41,680 --> 00:31:47,120
like this and but sometimes it's what we end up having to do. Like I said, the biggest mistake

312
00:31:47,120 --> 00:31:52,000
is not getting it over with before. people like this have unalterably damaged scouting for your

313
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:57,360
scouts. Danny wrote in with this question. he said: I'm looking for something more quote-unquote

314
00:31:57,360 --> 00:32:02,720
for our older boys. our older scouts seem disconnected. they seem to just want to play

315
00:32:02,720 --> 00:32:08,480
cards or go hide from the responsibilities most of the time. I'm hoping to change that soon.

316
00:32:08,480 --> 00:32:14,160
however, I'm wondering if I need some new program or some kind of carrot to get them fired up

317
00:32:14,160 --> 00:32:20,080
when the time comes. Well, my reply to Danny was: what do these scouts tell you when you discuss

318
00:32:20,080 --> 00:32:25,040
your concerns with them? if you sit down with them and you say: hey, fellas, looks to me like

319
00:32:25,040 --> 00:32:28,960
you're avoiding responsibility, just kind of sitting around playing cards, and I'm wondering what you

320
00:32:28,960 --> 00:32:34,159
think about that. and Danny replied: well, I'm not the scout master yet, so I haven't had that

321
00:32:34,159 --> 00:32:40,080
discussion. These boys have been led down the garden path about youth leadership some time ago,

322
00:32:40,080 --> 00:32:44,399
but things really didn't change on the part of the adults who were working with them.

323
00:32:44,399 --> 00:32:49,439
I'm just kind of putting my thoughts together and getting ideas for when this discussion happens,

324
00:32:49,440 --> 00:32:55,600
I will be the scout master and I'll be the fourth new scout master within a couple of years.

325
00:32:56,640 --> 00:33:02,800
My reply was: well. my best advice, then, is to keep observing and thinking like you're doing now

326
00:33:02,800 --> 00:33:09,200
and to do more of it when you become the scout master, but just to add to that, asking lots of

327
00:33:09,200 --> 00:33:14,000
questions. It's important that you have the best principles of scouting in your heart and mind,

328
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:20,800
and by what you've told me, you do, and it indicates to me that, if you're asking this question, you do,

329
00:33:20,800 --> 00:33:25,200
and that's more important than any kind of master plan that you're going to come up with

330
00:33:25,200 --> 00:33:30,080
When you become the scout master. what you want to do is get the scouts to come up with the

331
00:33:30,080 --> 00:33:35,600
master plan Now. that's not going to happen quickly and it's not going to change things overnight,

332
00:33:35,600 --> 00:33:40,160
but when you are in position to do so, you start asking questions and you get them to figure

333
00:33:40,160 --> 00:33:46,560
out what needs to change. You could roll in with a big plan and lots of new ideas,

334
00:33:46,560 --> 00:33:52,000
but you'd just be the next adult that stands there and tells them what's good for them.

335
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:57,360
Obviously, that's not really been working very well. because why? Because they're

336
00:33:57,360 --> 00:34:02,000
shirking responsibility, hiding out and playing cards. What will surprise you is

337
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:06,240
is that when you appeal to them for solutions, they're going to come up with

338
00:34:06,240 --> 00:34:11,120
things that you thought should happen anyway. If you let them discover these things

339
00:34:11,120 --> 00:34:16,080
instead of telling them all about it, it's going to be their idea and not yours, and

340
00:34:16,080 --> 00:34:20,560
they will voluntarily do what you thought you'd have to make them do.

341
00:34:20,560 --> 00:34:25,120
And to end the conversation, Danny replied: I absolutely agree, thank you. I didn't

342
00:34:25,120 --> 00:34:29,199
think about the fact that I'm just another guy who's going to tell them what he thinks.

343
00:34:29,199 --> 00:34:34,560
Instead, I'll just give them support and ride along with things, and I'll do my best to keep on

344
00:34:34,560 --> 00:34:38,960
track and lead them through the program. Well, that's the way that it's going to work.

345
00:34:38,960 --> 00:34:43,920
Like I said, it won't come along overnight, but if you work it this way and you let

346
00:34:43,920 --> 00:34:48,880
these changes originate with the scouts as soon as the changes start to take

347
00:34:48,880 --> 00:34:52,880
place, you won't be able to stop it. You won't be able to kill it, because it's

348
00:34:52,880 --> 00:34:56,639
something that they've come up with on their own and that they feel is

349
00:34:56,639 --> 00:35:00,480
important, rather than just something that the next adult, who happened to be the

350
00:35:00,480 --> 00:35:04,400
Scoutmaster, told them to do. Listen, if you have a question for me,

351
00:35:04,400 --> 00:35:07,840
I'd love to have you get in touch, and you're going to find out how to do that

352
00:35:07,840 --> 00:35:16,400
in just a moment.

